  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 311 PM CDT Tue Sep 8 2026\n\nWater vapor imagery currently shows some shortwave energy\ntracking across southern Manitoba with another circulation\napproaching the Northern Rockies. Meanwhile, upper ridging\ncontinues to span from the Southern Rockies through the\nSoutheast U.S. At the surface, cold front extends from northern\nIA, through eastern Nebraska and into northwest KS. \n\nStorms are expected to develop along the front late this\nafternoon into the early evening hours. While the bulk of this\nactivity will stay north of the forecast area there is chance a\ncouple storms could affect areas along and north of I-70. Main\nthreat with any storms that develop would be downburst winds,\ngiven decent DCAPE. \n\nUpper impulse over the Northern Rockies will continue tracking \neast and will slide across the Northern Plains tonight into Wed\nmorning. This will allow the front to continue pushing south \nand by early Wed afternoon will be near Highway 50. Storms are \nforecast to develop along and south of the front Wed afternoon \nas it continues to slowly work south. Strong upper jet is \nexpected to strengthen and sink south across the Mid Mississippi\nValley. This will place much of the area in the right entrance \nregion, which should help keep activity around Wed evening \nthrough Thu morning, especially across southern KS. We should \nsee a break in activity for Thu afternoon through the overnight \nhours as the shortwave moves into the Ohio Valley. \n\nNorthern stream will remain active as some additional upper\nenergy tracks across southern SK/MB and northern Plains on Fri\ninto Fri evening. This will allow another front to push through\nthe Northern/Central Plains. The bulk of the convection during \nthe day Fri looks to stay north of the forecast area, with both \nGFS and ECMWF tracking a complex of storms through central and \neastern KS Fri night into Sat morning. This will likely be the \nresult of storms developing along the front Fri evening in \nsouthern NE and diving southeast overnight. A more robust \nshortwave is then expected to dig into the northern Great Basin \nby early Sun morning and may provide some storm chances Sat \nnight into Sun morning as low level jet ramps-up and moisture \nworks back north. \n\nAs far as temps go, southern half of the area will likely see\nanother day of 100s on Wed. Even though temps will stay above\nnormal through the next 7 days, there's a good chance Wed could\nbe the last 100 degree day for most of the area.",
